1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:01,720
The Thermometer

2
00:00:03,359 --> 00:00:06,040
I can't believe the new thermometer isn't working.

3
00:00:06,879 --> 00:00:08,400
Tom Thomas, stay in bed.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,800
And I'll try and look for that old mercury thermometer.

5
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:17,280
Hey, did you get sick?

6
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:19,000
That's one way of saying it.

7
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:21,960
I don't know how I'm going to pass that math test today.

8
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:24,160
You're not ready, so you don't want to go to school.

9
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:25,519
Well, yeah.

10
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:29,560
So if you pull a sickie, then you can trick your mom!

11
00:00:29,559 --> 00:00:32,839
No, that's not true. I'm just pretending a little bit.

12
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:34,799
You think so?

13
00:00:34,879 --> 00:00:36,920
Well, you won't trick the thermometer!

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,560
Simka, what's a mercury thermometer?

15
00:00:43,119 --> 00:00:46,920
Mercury is a type of liquid metal that's silver in color.

16
00:00:47,079 --> 00:00:50,759
There's no mercury inside of new thermometers, now they're electronic.

17
00:00:51,399 --> 00:00:54,840
Old thermometers were made with a glass tube with markings

18
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,920
and a bit of mercury inside them.

19
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,120
When the end of the tube warms up,

20
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:03,160
the mercury inside of it expands and creeps up the tube.

21
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:06,079
And that's how those old thermometers measure temperature.

22
00:01:06,079 --> 00:01:10,359
The longer the column of mercury, the higher the person's temperature is.

23
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:16,799
That means I need to warm up the end of the thermometer.

24
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:19,480
Tom Thomas, you're a genius!

25
00:01:19,480 --> 00:01:21,120
But how will you warm it up?

26
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:22,799
Finally, I found it!

27
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:25,400
Well, let's see.

28
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,920
Mom, can I eat something?

29
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,719
Hang in there, sweetie. I'll make you something.

30
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:40,240
That is hot!

31
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:43,280
Now there's just no way it won't have the temperature!

32
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:46,160
Hey, what are you doing in here?

33
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:48,320
Well, how high did you get it?

34
00:01:48,439 --> 00:01:50,680
108 is what it's showing.

35
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:51,800
Oh no!

36
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:54,800
With the temperature that high, they'll send you straight to the hospital,

37
00:01:54,799 --> 00:01:56,039
and you don't need that.

38
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:58,200
You'd better shake that thermometer.

39
00:01:58,399 --> 00:01:59,719
Yeah, that's what I'll do.

40
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:01,799
That will get the temperature down a little.

41
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,000
Ah!

42
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:14,599
Well, so much for that!

43
00:02:14,719 --> 00:02:16,639
Cheaters never prosper!

44
00:02:16,919 --> 00:02:18,120
Tom Thomas!

45
00:02:18,319 --> 00:02:19,599
Did you see this?

46
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:21,439
Nolik, don't touch the mercury!

47
00:02:21,519 --> 00:02:22,879
It's poisonous!

48
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:24,519
Stop it right now!

49
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:27,360
And you, Tom Thomas, you don't touch that mercury either!

50
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:28,480
It's dangerous!

51
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:29,840
Then how can we throw it out?

52
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:31,680
Call your mom and she can help you.

53
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:32,840
I can't!

54
00:02:32,960 --> 00:02:34,080
How could I call her?

55
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:36,640
Then she'd find out that I wanted to trick her.

56
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,719
Maybe it's better to tell the truth.

57
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:40,960
I can't!

58
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:42,240
I can't do it!

59
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:44,840
Alright then, it looks like there's no other choice.

60
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:47,200
Nolik, call Papus and Masiya.

61
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:48,040
I'll get him!

62
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:50,160
And you go back to your room and wait.

63
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,100
Looks like this whole job is done.

64
00:03:21,099 --> 00:03:25,099
Not yet. We still need to neutralize this mercury.

65
00:03:28,099 --> 00:03:31,099
In everybody's home there's all sorts of chemicals around.

66
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:36,099
They are used for cleaning dishes, clothes, the bathroom and dealing with pests.

67
00:03:36,099 --> 00:03:39,099
And all of these substances can be very harmful to human health.

68
00:03:39,099 --> 00:03:42,099
But some people don't seem to understand this.

69
00:03:42,099 --> 00:03:48,099
They might use a dangerous spray or a poisonous liquid and then forget to wash their hands afterward.

70
00:03:48,819 --> 00:03:50,620
And then they go and eat!

71
00:03:50,819 --> 00:03:53,019
Or rub their eyes with their hands!

72
00:03:53,139 --> 00:03:56,979
That can cause serious damage to their vision or stomachs!

73
00:03:58,139 --> 00:04:01,139
And never put anything into your mouth that looks like medicine,

74
00:04:01,219 --> 00:04:03,579
unless your parents or a doctor gave it to you.

75
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:07,299
And if you ever happen to find something on the ground that looks like a piece of candy,

76
00:04:07,379 --> 00:04:09,099
you must never put it in your mouth!

77
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:10,939
You can get poisoned that way!

78
00:04:11,819 --> 00:04:13,459
Oh, humans!

79
00:04:13,579 --> 00:04:17,620
If they'd only remember this simple advice, they'd stay safer.

80
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:22,220
And what do we do with the glass that's broken?

81
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:24,220
That job's not for fixies.

82
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:28,760
Tom Thomas, we cleaned up all the mercury.

83
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:29,760
And the glass too?

84
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:31,480
No, not the broken glass.

85
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:32,200
But will you?

86
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,120
Papus said that it's not our job.

87
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:38,120
He told us you have to get your parents to come and help you.

88
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,960
That part's your responsibility.

89
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,860
Here's some food for you.

90
00:04:45,439 --> 00:04:46,280
What's the matter?

91
00:04:46,279 --> 00:04:50,319
Huh? Mom, I broke the thermometer.

92
00:04:50,679 --> 00:04:53,159
Broke it? Did you cut yourself?

93
00:04:53,359 --> 00:04:54,159
No.

94
00:04:54,319 --> 00:04:56,039
The mercury, did you touch it?

95
00:04:56,719 --> 00:04:57,719
I didn't.

96
00:04:58,119 --> 00:05:00,279
Simka, you think you'll tell her the truth?

97
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,639
And where did you break it?

98
00:05:03,439 --> 00:05:04,439
The bathroom.

99
00:05:04,719 --> 00:05:06,079
Why did you go in there?

100
00:05:06,719 --> 00:05:09,679
I wanted… I wanted to trick you.

101
00:05:10,279 --> 00:05:13,079
I have a test and I didn't study for it.

102
00:05:13,079 --> 00:05:16,240
And now it's too late for school!

